<com:TContent ID="body" >

<h1>Pages</h1>
<p class="block-content">
Pages are top-most controls that have no parent. The presentation of pages are directly displayed to end-users. Users access pages by sending  page service requests.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
Each page can have a <a href="?page=Configurations.Templates1">template</a> file. The file name suffix must be <tt>.page</tt>. The file name (without suffix) is the page name. PRADO will try to locate a page class file under the directory containing the page template file. Such a page class file must have the same file name (suffixed with <tt>.php</tt>) as the template file. If the class file is not found, the page will take class <tt>TPage</tt>.
</p>

<h2>PostBack</h2>
<p class="block-content">
A form submission is called <i>postback</i> if the submission is made to the page containing the form. Postback can be considered an event happened on the client side, raised by the user. PRADO will try to identify which control on the server side is responsible for a postback event. If one is determined, for example, a <tt>TButton</tt>, we call it the postback event sender which will translate the postback event into some specific server-side event (e.g. <tt>OnClick</tt> and <tt>OnCommand</tt> events for <tt>TButton</tt>).
<br/>
<tt>TPage</tt> has a <tt>IsPostBack</tt> property exposing whether the current request being handled is the first request for this page or the consequence of a postback.
</p>

<h2>CallBack</h2>
<p class="block-content">
A <i>callback</i> is a special form submission that, instead of requiring a full page reload on the browser, gets executed in the background through an ajax call. So, a callback is considered a postback too, but not vice versa.
<br/>
A callback is handled as a normal postback but, instead of re-rendering the entire page, only the specific changes occured on the page gets sent back to the client and merged with the current browser page. A typical callback response consists of:
</p>
<ol>
	<li>one or more pieces of html code that will replace existing content on the page;</li>
	<li>the javascript instructions needed to update the page;</li>
	<li>some specific fields used by prado to mantain the pagestate and add the needed external resources (stylesheets, javascript files, etc..).</li>
</ol>
<p class="block-content">
<tt>TPage</tt> has a <tt>IsCallBack</tt> property exposing whether the current request being handled is the consequence of a callback. Since a callback is also a postback, both <tt>IsPostBack</tt> and <tt>IsCallBack</tt> are true during callback requests.
</p>

<h2>Page Lifecycles</h2>
<p class="block-content">
Understanding the page lifecycle is crucial to grasp PRADO programming. The page
lifecycle is the ordered sequence of steps that a page runs through to serve a request.
Each step raises an event, and application and control code attaches handlers to these
events. The diagram shows the sequence.
</p>
<img src="<%~page-lifecycle.svg%>" class="figure" alt="The PRADO page lifecycle. TPageService raises onPreRunPage before the page runs, where modules attach handlers to reach the page and its request. The page then runs its own steps in order: onPreInit; onInit, which creates the child controls and the page hierarchy; onInitComplete; then for a postback loadPageState and processPostData; onPreLoad; onLoad, the main request handling; then for a postback processPostData, raiseChangedEvents, and raisePostBackEvent; onLoadComplete; onPreRender, the final preparation before rendering; onPreRenderComplete; onSaveState, which saves the view state and control state; onSaveStateComplete; renderControl, which renders the page hierarchy; and onUnload, which releases resources. The steps colored for all controls are raised on every control in the hierarchy, and the steps marked postback only run for a postback or callback request."/>
<p class="block-content">
The steps read from top to bottom. A step shown for all controls raises its event on every
control in the page hierarchy, so a control handles it by overriding the matching method,
such as <tt>onLoad</tt>. A step marked postback only runs when the request is a postback or
a callback, not for the first request for the page.
</p>

<h3>The onPreRunPage event</h3>
<com:SinceVersion Version="4.2.0" />
<p class="block-content">
<tt>TPageService</tt> raises the <tt>onPreRunPage</tt> event just before it runs the page,
after the page is created and its initial properties are set. A module attaches a handler
to this event to reach the page and its request as each page is created and processed.
This is a central extension point, because it gives a module the page instance for every
page request in the application.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
A module attaches the handler on the page service, usually in the module's <tt>init</tt>
method. The event sends the service as the sender and the page as the parameter.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content">
use Prado\Web\Services\TPageService;

// in the module's init():
$service = $this->getApplication()->getService();
if ($service instanceof TPageService) {
    $service->attachEventHandler('onPreRunPage', [$this, 'handlePage']);
}

// the handler receives the service and the page:
public function handlePage($sender, $page)
{
    // $page is the TPage that is about to run.
	// TPage is created but is at the start of its lifecycle
	//	without anything initialized.
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
Several core modules use this event. The
<a href="?page=Configurations.Parameters">database parameter</a> module attaches its
capture at <tt>onPreRunPage</tt>, so a change to an application parameter is written to the
database only after the page begins to run.
</p>

</com:TContent>
